<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude" xml:id="sec_4_2">


<title>Algebra of Powers: Fractional Exponents</title>

<introduction>
<objectives>
    <introduction>
        <p>
            After completing this section, you should be able to do the following.
        </p>
    </introduction>
    <ol>
        <li>
            <p>
            First objective.
            </p>
        </li>

        <li>
            <p>
        Second objective.
            </p>
        </li> 
        <li>
            <p>
            Third objective.
            </p>
        </li>
    </ol>
</objectives>

<p>
In this section, we will review the concepts of roots and radicals.
Please note that in this book the focus is on real numbers<mdash/>complex numbers are not considered.
In particular, when we talk about roots, we mean <em>real</em> roots.
</p>

</introduction>


<subsection>
<title>Roots and Radicals</title>

<p>
We say that a number <m>y</m> is a root of order <m>2</m> (a square root) of a number <m>a</m> if <m>y^2 = a</m>.
Similarly, we say that a number <m>y</m> is a root of order <m>3</m> (a cube root) of <m>a</m> if <m>y^3 = a</m>.
The definition of a root of order <m>n</m> is given below.
</p>

<definition>
<statement>
<p>
Let <m>a</m> be a given number and let <m>n</m> be a positive integer.
We say that <m>y</m> is a root of order <m>n</m> of <m>a</m>, or an <m>n</m>th root of <m>a</m>, if <m>y^n = a</m>.
</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<p>
For example, let <m>a = 4</m> and <m>n = 2</m>. The number <m>4</m> has two roots of order <m>2</m>: <m>y = 2</m> and <m>y = -2</m>, since <m>2^2 = (-2)^2 = 4</m>.
However, if <m>a = -4</m> and <m>n = 2</m>, the situation would be different.
The number <m>-4</m> has no roots of order <m>2</m>, since there are no real numbers whose square is <m>-4</m>.
We say that the square root of <m>-4</m> <em>does not exist</em>.
</p>

<p>
Now, take <m>a = -64</m> and <m>n = 3</m>. The number <m>-64</m> has <em>exactly one</em> root of order 3, which is <m>y = -4</m>. Indeed, <m>(-4)^3 = -64</m>. Observe that <m>4^3 = 64</m> so 4 is <em>not</em> a cube root of <m>-64</m>.
</p>

<theorem>
<title>Roots and Radicals</title>
<statement>
<p>
Let <m>a</m> be a given number and <m>n</m> be a positive integer.
</p>

<p>
    <ul>
        <li>
            <p>Suppose <m>n</m> is even.</p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                    If <m>a \lt 0</m>, then <m>a</m> has no <m>n</m>th order roots.
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                    If <m>a \gt 0</m>, then <m>a</m> has two <m>n</m>th roots, one positive and one of the same magnitude but negative. We denote the <term>positive</term> <m>n</m>th root as:
                    <md>\sqrt[n]{a}</md>
                    (We use the <q>radical</q> symbol <m>\sqrt{\,\,}</m> to denote a root.)
                    The two <m>n</m>th roots can then be written as
                    <md>\sqrt[n]{a} \hspace{15pt} \text{and} \hspace{12pt}  -\sqrt[n]{a}
                    .</md>
                    </p>
                    <aside>
                    <p>
                    We often use the notation <m>\pm \sqrt[n]{a}</m> to denote the positive and negative roots.
                    </p>
                    </aside>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </li>

        <li>
        Suppose <m>n</m> is odd. Then <m>a</m> has exactly one <m>n</m>th root which we denote as <md>\sqrt[n]{a}.</md> If <m>a</m> is negative, the root is negative; if <m>a</m> is positive the root is positive.
        </li>

        <li>
        If <m>a = 0</m>, then <m>a</m> has one root of any order: <m>\sqrt[n]{0} = 0</m>.
        </li>
    </ul>
</p>
</statement>
</theorem>

<aside>
<p>
When <m>n=2</m>, instead of writing <m>\sqrt[2]{a}</m> we simply drop the <m>2</m> and write <m>\sqrt{a}</m>. This is the only exception to this notation.
</p>
</aside>



<example>
<statement>
<p>
Find all (real) roots specified below and write them in terms of radicals.
</p>

<p><ol>
<li>
    All roots of order <m>4</m> of <m>81</m>
</li>

<li>
    All roots of order <m>2</m> of <m>7</m>
</li>

<li>
    All roots of order <m>3</m> of <m>-27</m>
</li>

<li>
    All roots of order <m>4</m> of <m>-81</m>
</li>

</ol></p>
</statement>

<solution>
<p><ol>
<li>
<p>
We are looking for all numbers <m>y</m> such that <m>y^4 = 81</m>. The order, <m>4</m>, is even. Hence, we have two roots, one positive and one negative. The positive root is denoted by <m>\sqrt[4]{81}</m> and the two roots are: <md>\sqrt[4]{81} \hspace{10pt} \text{and} \hspace{10pt} -\sqrt[4]{81}.</md> The positive number whose <m>4</m>th power is <m>81</m> is <m>3</m>. That is: <md>\sqrt[4]{81} = 3.</md> Hence, the two roots of order <m>4</m> of <m>81</m> are <m>3</m> and <m>-3</m>. Indeed, <m>3^4 = (-3)^4 = 81</m>.
</p>
</li>

<li>
<p>
The number <m>7</m> has two roots of order <m>2</m>: <m>\sqrt{7}</m> and <m>-\sqrt{7}</m>.
We cannot easily guess them as they are not integers.
Using a calculator, though, <m>\sqrt{7} \approx 2.65</m>.
The two roots are then approximately <m>2.65</m> and <m>-2.65</m>.
</p>
</li>

<li>
<p>
The order, <m>3</m>, is odd. Hence, there is only one root of order <m>3</m> of <m>-27</m> denoted as <m>\sqrt[3]{-27}</m>. As <m>(-3)^3 = -27</m>, we have <md>\sqrt[3]{-27} = -3.</md>
</p>
</li>

<li>
<p>
A negative number <m>a = -81</m> has no roots of even order, and <m>4</m> is even. Hence, <m>-81</m> has no roots of order <m>4</m>.
</p>
</li>

</ol></p>
</solution>

</example>




<p>
By definition, <m>x</m> is a root of order <m>n</m> of <m>a</m> if <m>x</m> is a solution to the equation: <md>x^n = a</md> Hence, roots and radicals appear naturally when solving equations containing powers of the unknown.
You may recall seeing many radicals in Chapter 3 in the context of quadratic equations.
</p>

<example>
<statement>
<p>
Solve for <m>x</m>. Find all (real) solutions and give their exact as well as approximate values.
</p>

<p><ol>
<li>
<p>
    <m>2x^2 - 5 = 7</m>
</p>
</li>

<li>
<p>
    <m>-3x^3 = 27</m>
</p>
</li>

<li>
<p>
    <m>2x^4 + 3 = 4</m>
</p>
</li>

<li>
<p>
    <m>2(x - 1)^2 + 3 = 11</m>
</p>
</li>

</ol></p>
</statement>

<solution>
<p><ol>
<li>
<p>
We add <m>5</m> to both sides of the equation and then divide both sides by <m>2</m> to obtain <md>x^2 = 6.</md> Solutions to the equation are roots of order <m>2</m> of <m>6</m>. There are two such roots: <md>x = \sqrt{6}\approx 2.449 \hspace{10pt} \text{and}  \hspace{10pt} x = -\sqrt{6} \approx -2.449.</md>
</p>
</li>

<li>
<p>
We divide both sides of the equation by <m>-3</m> and obtain: <md>x^3 = -9.</md> There is one cube root of <m>-9</m>. Hence, our equation has one solution: <md>x = \sqrt[3]{-9} \approx -2.08.</md>
</p>
</li>

<li>
<p>
We subtract <m>3</m> from both sides of the equation, then divide by <m>2</m>. The equation becomes: <md>x^4 = 0.5.</md> We are looking for all roots of order <m>4</m> of <m>0.5</m>. As <m>4</m> is even, we have two such roots: <md>x = \sqrt[4]{0.5} \approx  0.841 \hspace{10pt} \text{and}  \hspace{10pt} x = -\sqrt[4]{0.5} \approx -0.841.</md>
</p>
</li>

<li>
<p>
We begin by <q>solving</q> for <m>(x - 1)</m>. Subtract <m>3</m> from both sides and divide both sides by <m>2</m>: <md>(x - 1)^2 = 4.</md> Hence, <m>(x - 1)</m> is a root of order 2 of 4. There are two such roots: <md>(x - 1) = \sqrt{4} \hspace{10pt} \text{and}  \hspace{10pt} (x - 1) = -\sqrt{4}.</md> This gives <m>x - 1 = 2</m> and <m>x - 1 = -2</m>. We solve each of the two equations for <m>x</m> and obtain two solutions: <md>x = 3 \hspace{10pt} \text{and}  \hspace{10pt} x = -1.</md>
</p>
</li>

</ol></p>
</solution>
</example>



<p>
    Here are a few important properties of roots.
</p>

<theorem>
<title>Properties of Radicals</title>
<statement>
    <p>
        Let <m>a</m>, <m>b</m> be given numbers. Let <m>n</m>, <m>m</m> be positive integers. Then the following equalities hold provided that the roots involved exist, and both sides are defined:
    </p>

    <p><ul>
        <li>
                        <p>
                            <m>\displaystyle \sqrt[n]{a \cdot b} = \sqrt[n]{a} \cdot \sqrt[n]{b}</m>
                        </p>
        </li>

        <li>
                        <p>
                            <m>\displaystyle \sqrt[n]{\frac{a}{b}} = \frac{\sqrt[n]{a}}{\sqrt[n]{b}}</m>
                        </p>
        </li>

        <li>
                        <p>
                            <m>\displaystyle (\sqrt[n]{a})^m = \sqrt[n]{a^m}</m>
                        </p>
        </li>

        <li>
                        <p>
                            <m>\displaystyle \sqrt[n]{a^n} = |a|</m> if <m>n</m> is even
                        </p>
        </li>

        <li>
                        <p>
                            <m>\displaystyle \sqrt[n]{a^n} = a</m> if <m>n</m> is odd
                        </p>
        </li>

    </ul></p>
</statement>
</theorem>

</subsection>




<subsection>
<title>Powers with Fractional Exponents and Arbitrary Exponents</title>

<p>
    So far, we have defined powers <m>a^p</m> for all <term>integer</term> exponents <m>p</m>.
</p>

<p>
To extend the definition to fractional exponents <m>a^{\frac{m}{n}}</m>, we will use roots. In the first step, for every positive integer <m>n</m>, we define <m>a^{\frac{1}{n}}</m> as: <md>a^{\frac{1}{n}} = \sqrt[n]{a}</md> Does this make sense?
Recall that <m>\left(\sqrt[n]{a}\right)^n = a</m>.
Therefore, <md>(a^{\frac{1}{n}})^n = a</md> which is what Rules of Exponents would dictate. In the next step, we define <m>a^{\frac{m}{n}} = (a^{\frac{1}{n}})^m = (\sqrt[n]{a})^m</m>.
Here is a precise definition of a power with a fractional exponent.
</p>

<definition>
<statement>
<p>
Let <m>a</m> be a given number and let <m>m</m> and <m>n</m> be positive integers. Assume that <m>\sqrt[n]{a}</m> exists. We define:
</p>
<p>
<md>
<mrow>a^{\frac{1}{n}} \amp = \sqrt[n]{a} </mrow>
<mrow>a^{\frac{m}{n}} \amp = (\sqrt[n]{a})^m = \sqrt[n]{a^m} </mrow>
</md>
</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<p>
Using properties of exponents, we also have
<md>
a^{-\frac{m}{n}} = \frac{1}{a^{m/n}} \hspace{7pt} \text{provided} \hspace{7pt} a \neq 0.
</md>
</p>

<p>
    Note that <m>\sqrt[n]{a}</m> exists unless <m>n</m> is even and <m>a</m> is negative. The combination of negative radicands (numbers under radicals) with even roots causes a number of problems for the behavior of fractional exponents, and the Rules of Exponents do not always hold.
</p>

<p>
For example, is the following equality <em>always</em> true?
<md>\left( a^{\frac{1}{n}} \right)^m = \left( a^m \right)^{\frac{1}{n}}</md>
It seems reasonable at first, but consider the following:
<md>\left( (-4)^{\frac{1}{4}} \right)^2 \quad \text{and} \quad \left( (-4)^2 \right)^{\frac{1}{4}}.</md> The first number is <m>\left( (-4)^{\frac{1}{4}} \right)^2 = (\sqrt[4]{-4})^2</m> which is undefined. However, <md>\left( (-4)^2 \right)^{\frac{1}{4}} = 16^{\frac{1}{4}} = \sqrt[4]{16} = 2.</md>
Therefore, when we talk about fractional powers, we will most often assume that bases are positive except for some simple cases where no issues arise, such as <md>a^{\frac{1}{3}} = \sqrt[3]{a}.</md>
</p>

<example>
<statement>
    <p>
        Find:
    </p>

<p><ol>
<li>
    <m>\displaystyle 4^{\frac{1}{2}}</m>
</li>

<li>
    <m>\displaystyle (-4)^{\frac{1}{2}}</m>
</li>

<li>
    <m>\displaystyle 27^{\frac{1}{3}}</m>
</li>

<li>
    <m>\displaystyle (-27)^{\frac{1}{3}}</m>
</li>

<li>
    <m>\displaystyle (1700)^{\frac{1}{20}}</m>
</li>
</ol></p>
</statement>

<solution>

<p><ol>
<li>
    By definition <m>4^{\frac{1}{2}} = \sqrt[2]{4} = \sqrt{4} = 2</m>. Note that <m>4^{\frac{1}{2}}</m> is the <term>positive</term> of the two square roots of <m>4</m> as is <m>\sqrt{4}</m>.
</li>

<li>
    <m>(-4)^{\frac{1}{2}} = \sqrt{-4}</m> is undefined. An even-order root of a negative number is undefined. Hence, <m>(-4)^{\frac{1}{2}}</m> is undefined.
</li>

<li>
    <m>27^{\frac{1}{3}} = \sqrt[3]{27} = 3</m>.
</li>

<li>
    <m>(-27)^{\frac{1}{3}} = \sqrt[3]{-27} = -3</m>.
</li>

<li>
<p>
    <m>\displaystyle (1700)^{\frac{1}{20}} = \sqrt[20]{1700} \approx 1.451</m>.
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>

</solution>
</example>

<aside>
<p>
    Depending on your calculator, it may be easier to calculate a fractional power than to enter the corresponding radical. You can simply enter <c>1700^(1/20)</c>. Be sure to use parentheses!
</p>
</aside>



<p>
We have defined powers <m>a^p</m> for integer and fractional exponents <m>p</m>. As you may know, not all real numbers can be expressed as fractions (irrational numbers, for example). Can we define powers <m>a^p</m> for all real numbers <m>p</m>? The answer is yes, provided the base <m>a</m> is positive. The construction falls outside the scope of this course. It suffices to know that <m>a^p</m> can be defined for all exponents and Rules of Exponents are preserved. Therefore, we have the following result.
</p>

<theorem>
<title>Rules of Exponents <mdash/> Arbitrary Exponents</title>
<statement>
<p>
    Let <m>a</m>, <m>b</m> be given <term>positive</term> numbers, <m>p</m> and <m>r</m> be real numbers. Then the following equalities hold:
</p>

<p><ol>
<li>
    <m>\displaystyle a^{-p} = \frac{1}{a^p}</m>
</li>

<li>
    <m>\displaystyle a^p \cdot a^r = a^{p + r}</m>
</li>

<li>
    <m>\displaystyle \frac{a^p}{a^r} = a^{p - r}</m>
</li>

<li>
    <m>\displaystyle (a^p)^r = a^{p \cdot r}</m>
</li>

<li>
    <m>\displaystyle (a \cdot b)^p = a^p \cdot b^p</m>
</li>

<li>
    <m>\displaystyle \left( \frac{a}{b} \right)^p = \frac{a^p}{b^p}</m>
</li>

<li>
    <m>\displaystyle \left( \frac{a}{b} \right)^{-p} = \left( \frac{b}{a} \right)^{p}</m>
</li>

</ol></p>
</statement>
</theorem>

<example>
<statement>
<p>
Rewrite each of the following expressions as a power of 5; that is, in the form <m>5^p</m> for some <m>p</m>.
</p>

<p><ol>
<li>
<p>
    <m>\displaystyle \sqrt[3]{5}</m>
</p>
</li>

<li>
<p>
    <m>\displaystyle 5\sqrt{5}</m>
</p>
</li>

<li>
<p>
    <m>\displaystyle \frac{5\sqrt{5}}{25}</m>
</p>
</li>

<li>
<p>
    <m>\displaystyle \left( \frac{5}{\sqrt[3]{5}} \right)^2</m>
</p>
</li>

</ol></p>
</statement>

<solution>
<p><ol>
<li>
<p>
By definition of fractional powers, <md>\sqrt[3]{5} = 5^{\frac{1}{3}}.</md>
</p>
</li>

<li>
<p>
By definition of fractional powers and the properties above,
<md>
5\sqrt{5}
=
5 \cdot 5^{\frac{1}{2}}
\overset{<xref ref="int-exps-prop2">Property</xref>}{=}
5^{1 + \frac{1}{2}}
=
5^{\frac{3}{2}}.
</md>
</p>
</li>

<li>
<p>
Use the fact that <m>25 = 5^2</m> to simplify the expression:
<md>
\frac{5\sqrt{5}}{25}
=
\frac{5 \cdot 5^{\frac{1}{2}}}{5^2}
\overset{<xref ref="int-exps-prop2">Property</xref>}{=}
\frac{5^{\frac{3}{2}}}{5^2}
\overset{<xref ref="int-exps-prop4">Property</xref>}{=}
5^{\frac{3}{2} - 2}
=
5^{-\frac{1}{2}}.</md>
</p>
</li>

<li>
<p>
Note that <m>\sqrt[3]{5} = 5^{\frac{1}{3}}</m>.
Therefore:
<md>
\left( \frac{5}{\sqrt[3]{5}} \right)^2
=
\left( \frac{5}{5^{\frac{1}{3}}} \right)^2
\overset{<xref ref="int-exps-prop3">Property</xref>}{=}
\left( 5^{\frac{2}{3}} \right)^2
\overset{<xref ref="int-exps-prop4">Property</xref>}{=}
5^{\frac{2}{3} \cdot 2}
=
5^{\frac{4}{3}}.</md>
</p>
</li>

</ol></p>
</solution>

</example>





<example>
<statement>
<p>
Use Rules of Exponents to simplify the following. Write your answers in terms of powers and not radicals. Assume <m>a</m>, <m>b</m>, <m>x</m>, and <m>y</m> are all positive.
</p>

<p><ol>
<li>
<p>
    <m>\displaystyle \sqrt{\dfrac{36}{a^5}}</m>
</p>
</li>

<li>
<p>
    <m>\displaystyle \sqrt[3]{8x^2y^6}</m>
</p>
</li>

<li>
<p>
    <m>\displaystyle \dfrac{b^{\frac{3}{2}}\sqrt{a^3}}{ba^2}</m>
</p>
</li>

<li>
<p>
    <m>\displaystyle \dfrac{14x^{-2}}{21\sqrt{x^7}}</m>
</p>
</li>

</ol></p>
</statement>


<solution>
<p><ol>
<li>
<p>
<m>\displaystyle \sqrt{\frac{36}{a^5}} = \frac{\sqrt{36}}{\sqrt{a^5}} = \frac{6}{a^{5/2}} = 6a^{-(5/2)}.</m>
</p>
</li>

<li>
<p>
First, compute
<md>
\sqrt[3]{8x^2y^6} = \sqrt[3]{8} \cdot \sqrt[3]{x^2} \cdot \sqrt[3]{y^6} = 2 \cdot (x^2)^{1/3} \cdot (y^6)^{1/3}
.</md>
Applying properties of exponents:
<md>
2 \cdot x^{2/3} \cdot y^{6/3} = 2x^{2/3}y^2
</md>.
</p>
</li>

<li>
<p>
Applying rules of exponents directly yields:
<md>
\frac{b^{\frac{3}{2}}\sqrt{a^3}}{ba^2} = \frac{b^{\frac{3}{2}} a^{\frac{3}{2}}}{b a^2} = b^{\frac{3}{2} - 1} a^{\frac{3}{2} - 2} = b^{1/2}a^{-(1/2)}.
</md>
</p>
</li>

<li>
<p>
First, observe that <m>14 = 2 \cdot 7</m> and <m>21 = 3 \cdot 7</m>.
This allows us to simplify the fraction <m>14/21</m> as <m>2/3</m>.
Since <m>x^{-2} = \dfrac{1}{x^2}</m> and <m>\sqrt{x^7} = x^{7/2}</m>, then 
<md>
\frac{14x^{-2}}{21\sqrt{x^7}} = \frac{2}{3x^2x^{7/2}} = \frac{2}{3}x^{-(11/2)}.
</md>
</p>
</li>

</ol></p>
</solution>

</example>


</subsection>




<xi:include href="exercises/ex_4_2.ptx"/>
</section>